WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:06.080
 Well, welcome, Jon. It's great having you here with

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.073
us. And we'll be hearing from you very shortly in your distinguished lecture.

00:00:09.073 --> 00:00:11.238
But we take a little time out to say hello a little more

00:00:11.238 --> 00:00:16.267
informally at our fireside chat without the fire today.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:20.746
Anyway, just a little bit maybe about your background and how you got interested

00:00:20.746 --> 00:00:24.027
in computer science and where did that interest come from to start with?

00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:29.646
 Let's see. So, yeah, I guess where the interest came from -- in high school I was,

00:00:29.646 --> 00:00:35.004
I think I sort of wanted to be a math major. I mean, actually from some early

00:00:35.004 --> 00:00:39.065
phase I either wanted to be like a math major or fiction writer or a lawyer,

00:00:39.065 --> 00:00:43.949
I think, but gradually it was all sort of coalesced towards something more technical. Also that was

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:49.192
kind of the era when the Apple 2 Plus had just appeared and I liked writing programs in the Apple 2

00:00:49.192 --> 00:00:53.422
plus and I liked writing games my friends could play in the school library on floppy disks and things,

00:00:53.422 --> 00:00:56.166
but I somehow didn't imagine that was like a field of study, it was

00:00:56.166 --> 00:01:01.788
just a hobby you do. So the striking thing was when I got to college,

00:01:01.788 --> 00:01:06.811
as an undergrad at Cornell to discover there was actually an academic field of computer

00:01:06.811 --> 00:01:10.941
science with some rather inspirational faculty who are teaching courses in it.

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:16.510
I ended up double majoring in math and computer science. And I think my

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:21.079
interest in computer science was sort of toward the theoretical end of things,

00:01:21.079 --> 00:01:30.414
my interest in math. But then sort of in the mid-90s, 1996, '97 after grad

00:01:30.414 --> 00:01:37.490
school, I was doing a post-doc at IBM at Almaden with Prabhakar Raghavan.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:44.128
And he made the argument, this amazing opportunity, the

00:01:44.128 --> 00:01:47.280
Web, sort of like the personal computer in the '80s,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.991
seems like a glorified hobby that people were playing around with, but there's a lot going on here.

00:01:50.991 --> 00:01:54.488
I began thinking about questions about organizing information

00:01:54.488 --> 00:01:59.372
on the Web. And with Eva Tardos in grad school,

00:01:59.372 --> 00:02:03.253
I had done so much work on graph algorithms. I thought one

00:02:03.253 --> 00:02:05.892
thing I contribute here is my understanding of graphs.

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:12.034
And so thinking about the length structure of the Web made me think you

00:02:12.034 --> 00:02:15.066
really need to understand the human system to understand what's going on.

00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:19.295
And the plan for me was really a lot of fun, there was a

00:02:19.295 --> 00:02:21.482
mathematical aspect thinking about the network structure.

00:02:21.482 --> 00:02:27.439
But it tapped into all of these other sort of fascinations I had from early on

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:32.317
in high school with narrative and law and policy and social science and all of

00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:35.904
that showed up in the Web and you have yet to understand all these things.

00:02:35.904 --> 00:02:40.219
I think somehow I ended up in this mix of topics that I find interesting now.

00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:43.756
 That actually was a really good explanation of what

00:02:43.756 --> 00:02:48.052
others might see as a Venn diagram with some interesting intersections.

00:02:48.052 --> 00:02:57.211
I remember reading your Hubs and Authority work talking about Web, structure of the Web back in the

00:02:57.211 --> 00:03:06.175
'90s and thinking about what this might mean for both the science for understanding the mixture of

00:03:06.175 --> 00:03:14.960
human interests and human generated content and human interest in accessing that content and the

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:24.421
algorithms that we could actually sort of use to capture and to optimize various kinds of objectives.

00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:32.381
Curious to hear your reflection about, describe more about what you were doing with the Hubs

00:03:32.381 --> 00:03:37.944
and Authority work and what that meant you think for the evolution of thinking about the Web.

00:03:37.944 --> 00:03:43.811
 Right. So, I mean, when I first started thinking about the Web, it was --

00:03:43.811 --> 00:03:49.212
fundamentally it seemed like it was an information retrieval problem on a giant text corpus.

00:03:49.212 --> 00:03:54.091
We put a lot of documents out there and we want to find the documents that have the terms that we want.

00:03:54.091 --> 00:04:00.217
But when I began thinking about the kinds of queries I myself was trying to solve and

00:04:00.217 --> 00:04:06.423
actually at the time sort of summer of '96 I was moving for a year to a new place San Jose,

00:04:06.423 --> 00:04:09.776
I now live in California. So you're doing a lot of research

00:04:09.776 --> 00:04:13.398
about where can I buy this thing from my apartment.

00:04:13.398 --> 00:04:18.666
And so you're constantly on the Web and it wasn't -- it was often the activity was to try to

00:04:18.666 --> 00:04:23.175
sort of organize this giant mass of information to figure out what are the good sources.

00:04:23.175 --> 00:04:29.075
And so I began to discover a thing I myself was doing as I chased down the length structure

00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:34.220
and engage in some meta awareness of what am I doing as I try to buy furniture for my apartment

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:39.718
or try to find what are the reading groups that are meeting about different things.

00:04:39.718 --> 00:04:44.389
And it was clear that the information about which were the important pages was not intrinsic to the page.

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:46.772
When you found the right page it wasn't that it said the

00:04:46.772 --> 00:04:48.887
word you wanted more often or in bigger font or anything.

00:04:48.887 --> 00:04:52.149
It was you only knew it was the right answer because all the things that pointed to

00:04:52.149 --> 00:04:56.803
it. That was the argument that links were somehow providing this crucial evidence.

00:04:56.803 --> 00:05:02.246
But the other thing that was happening was that there really was this two-sided nature to it.

00:05:02.246 --> 00:05:08.532
Because there were these good pages. But once we found a few pages you

00:05:08.532 --> 00:05:11.202
realized that there were these other pages that had linked to all of them.

00:05:11.202 --> 00:05:14.100
You sort of missed them on your first pass; but in retrospect

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.876
there was this one page that had you listen to it.

00:05:15.876 --> 00:05:20.836
It actually listed the three things you ended up finding interesting. It wasn't just that,

00:05:20.836 --> 00:05:23.319
it actually listed six things. Now that I'm taking that page

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:25.683
more seriously, I should figure out what the other three were.

00:05:25.683 --> 00:05:28.711
Had the top three, maybe these other three were great also. These were

00:05:28.711 --> 00:05:32.308
these really powerful Web pages and somebody had done the work before you.

00:05:32.308 --> 00:05:33.967
 To create a hub.

00:05:33.967 --> 00:05:37.267
 It was someone who had been faced roughly with the same task you

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:40.300
had and they had done a very good solving and documenting their solution.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.931
Let's go back and learn from them. So once we find a good

00:05:43.931 --> 00:05:47.028
hub, certain things now seem like better authorities.

00:05:47.028 --> 00:05:53.211
And you say, okay, they're good authorities, good hubs linked to authorities.

00:05:53.211 --> 00:05:56.435
And at that point as a computer scientist you think we should iterate this.

00:05:56.435 --> 00:06:01.432
If we have better estimates for our authorities we should find better estimates for our hubs.

00:06:01.432 --> 00:06:07.667
And the appealing thing is that the iteration had some very natural structure and it exposed this

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:13.894
two-sided nature to all of these topics that there was the thing itself and then there were the pages

00:06:13.894 --> 00:06:19.441
that all linked to the thing itself and in some ways served as the glue that held it all together.

00:06:19.441 --> 00:06:24.041
 This idea about this human effort to organize that's represented implicitly

00:06:24.041 --> 00:06:29.309
in lots of activity on the Web and there's stuff being pointed at as the gold mine,

00:06:29.309 --> 00:06:33.239
the gold nuggets information that someone else has put there because it's the information itself.

00:06:33.239 --> 00:06:40.333
 Exactly. And in a way it's like we can all be basketball fans, for example, and we can all --

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:43.274
we're all unified by the fact we all refer to the same basketball

00:06:43.274 --> 00:06:46.340
stars even if we never directly interact with the basketball stars,

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.665
their existence somehow serves to create a community among us.

00:06:48.665 --> 00:06:55.170
 Right. It seems that those ideas were so nascent in the

00:06:55.170 --> 00:07:02.809
mid to late 90s coming in part to all the contributions you were making.

00:07:02.809 --> 00:07:08.061
In advance of like page rank and other structural analyses that people now, they

00:07:08.061 --> 00:07:12.740
say are core even what's going on in these algorithms anymore are sort of at the

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.012
root of how we look at Web and how we look at information retrieval and indexing,

00:07:16.012 --> 00:07:21.310
which is interesting. Now you had a comment there? I'll say that

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:25.062
the recent work you're doing I think most recently in some ways,

00:07:25.062 --> 00:07:29.609
maybe last couple of years, of course you do many things. But I've

00:07:29.609 --> 00:07:35.608
been very fascinated to see you bringing together policy law,

00:07:35.608 --> 00:07:42.133
social science in issues around fairness which have come to the fore lately when people rightly

00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:47.793
concerned about systematic biases in tools that might amplify them through great use.

00:07:47.793 --> 00:07:52.669
And you want to talk about the work, includes criminal justice, for example.

00:07:52.669 --> 00:07:59.819
 Absolutely. This is something that I started thinking about sort of around 2011,

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:04.299
2012 and actually it began with a series of conversations with

00:08:04.299 --> 00:08:07.530
Sendhil Mullainathan, a behavioral economist at Harvard.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:11.249
We had been undergrads together at Cornell.

00:08:11.249 --> 00:08:12.363
 I didn't know that.

00:08:12.363 --> 00:08:16.904
 Yeah, we actually have -- my second paper and his first

00:08:16.904 --> 00:08:21.733
paper was actually a PodC 1993 paper that came out of a joint,

00:08:21.733 --> 00:08:27.265
class project jointly we did in Keith Marzul's operating systems class. We submitted it.

00:08:27.265 --> 00:08:30.174
 I would never even have connected Keith to both of you

00:08:30.174 --> 00:08:33.850
at the time. Thanks for making that little meta link for me now.

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.859
 Exactly. It's a feature. We both took this really inspiring class

00:08:36.859 --> 00:08:40.715
from him and just poured a lot of effort into doing a class project for him.

00:08:40.715 --> 00:08:46.826
And at the time it's funny that Sendhil was -- Dick Saylor was at Cornell at the

00:08:46.826 --> 00:08:50.814
time and Sendhil was very excited about these ideas in behavioral economics.

00:08:50.814 --> 00:08:56.025
And we went our separate ways professionally. And starting,

00:08:56.025 --> 00:08:59.667
2011 and 2012, it was Sendhil who initiated the contact,

00:08:59.667 --> 00:09:03.567
and at this point we're thinking about very similar things because he had been

00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:08.088
very focused on human decision-making and human experts and high stakes settings

00:09:08.088 --> 00:09:11.959
and even low stake settings and the kinds of systematic errors that people make.

00:09:11.959 --> 00:09:16.149
And I was thinking about algorithms which are decision-making entities. We're

00:09:16.149 --> 00:09:20.120
now in these situations where we can ask what's the interface between --

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:26.366
and it did feel like around 2011 and 12 there was this missing subject where we had human

00:09:26.366 --> 00:09:31.205
computer interaction but there was sort of this emerging human machine learning interaction,

00:09:31.205 --> 00:09:35.306
how do humans interact with big data resources and the algorithms that make predictions based on that.

00:09:35.306 --> 00:09:38.824
 The human AI interaction more broadly.

00:09:38.824 --> 00:09:39.457
 Broadly. Probably absolutely.

00:09:39.457 --> 00:09:41.336
 Please go on.

00:09:41.336 --> 00:09:45.562
 Absolutely. I think of those all bundled together at this point.

00:09:45.562 --> 00:09:53.265
And so we looked at domains where the situation called for a prediction.

00:09:53.265 --> 00:09:57.456
The idea being there are lots of decisions that human beings make and

00:09:57.456 --> 00:09:59.331
it's a fascinating question to ask which of these can be automated,

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:03.991
which ones can't. But let's do it on the cases where what people are doing is making predictions

00:10:03.991 --> 00:10:08.520
and asking what we can learn about the human process and also whether there are gains to be had.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:16.375
And so we start talking to Jens Ludwig, a public policy researcher at the University of Chicago,

00:10:16.375 --> 00:10:23.646
one of whose domains is crime policy. He had these interesting datasets on judges making bail decisions.

00:10:23.646 --> 00:10:29.084
And the reason to look at bail, if you look at criminal justice, obviously one of the highest

00:10:29.084 --> 00:10:33.873
stakes settings where experts are making decision that have enormous impact on people's lives,

00:10:33.873 --> 00:10:36.257
if you look at the pipeline of decisions that get made there,

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:39.857
decisions about arrest, decisions about pretrial detention and bail,

00:10:39.857 --> 00:10:45.845
present at trial, sentencing, parole, many, many things, if you ask where in that

00:10:45.845 --> 00:10:49.348
pipeline is a person doing something that most resembles a pure prediction task,

00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:53.748
at least according to the law that's arguably on bail, because what the law says a judge

00:10:53.748 --> 00:10:58.178
should do on bail is to release the person before trial if they believe with high probability

00:10:58.178 --> 00:11:01.570
they'll return for their court appearance without committing a crime in the meantime.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.154
They shouldn't be thinking about their eventual guilt or innocence shouldn't be

00:11:04.154 --> 00:11:07.915
thinking about what's the incentive effect of my decision on future defendants,

00:11:07.915 --> 00:11:11.517
you should be making that prediction. Now there's a whole important

00:11:11.517 --> 00:11:15.249
set of questions about are they making exactly that prediction.

00:11:15.249 --> 00:11:19.496
But there's at least a case where we could come in with the data we have and [indiscernible].

00:11:19.496 --> 00:11:25.286
So this was an appealing problem. I roped in Jure Leskovec, my former post-doc, and his student,

00:11:25.286 --> 00:11:28.611
Teema [inaudible], who is graduating right now.

00:11:28.611 --> 00:11:34.308
 Jure is a former intern here at Microsoft Research. Really appreciated sharing his intellect.

00:11:34.308 --> 00:11:39.608
 Yeah. He's been an enormous pleasure to work with now

00:11:39.608 --> 00:11:44.422
over a decade. And so the five of us set on this project,

00:11:44.422 --> 00:11:47.535
which actually took a number of years, really started this some

00:11:47.535 --> 00:11:51.317
summer, got going with the five us in the summer of 2013.

00:11:51.317 --> 00:12:01.841
And so there were a lot of challenges here. So as you said, one challenge is that the data is biased.

00:12:01.841 --> 00:12:07.131
If we think about this as a prediction problem, there's features and there's a label. So the

00:12:07.131 --> 00:12:12.559
features are maybe the administrative data that we have on past arrests for what types of crimes,

00:12:12.559 --> 00:12:15.588
past convictions, what was the status of the person at the time of the arrest.

00:12:15.588 --> 00:12:19.776
Information that the algorithm was able to see in some kind of tabular form.

00:12:19.776 --> 00:12:23.673
And then the label is if they got released did they return

00:12:23.673 --> 00:12:25.908
for their court appearance without committing a crime.

00:12:25.908 --> 00:12:30.717
On the feature side, of course, the administrative data is contaminated

00:12:30.717 --> 00:12:35.237
by incredibly differential rates of policing and in different areas.

00:12:35.237 --> 00:12:38.330
And so an arrest does not necessarily mean the same thing

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:40.415
in all different areas and per unit level of crime,

00:12:40.415 --> 00:12:44.178
it doesn't mean the same things. And that becomes a big issue. It's very

00:12:44.178 --> 00:12:48.128
much in the spirit of what people worry about when we say we're taking

00:12:48.128 --> 00:12:51.299
biases that already came and already predated this moment in the courtroom.

00:12:51.299 --> 00:12:56.098
On the label, this notion of predicting crime while they're released, we don't have a label of crime,

00:12:56.098 --> 00:13:00.658
we have a label arrest. All we can do is see if they were arrested. Even

00:13:00.658 --> 00:13:04.908
failure to appear has a lot of correlation with socioeconomic status.

00:13:04.908 --> 00:13:08.610
So there are issues both on the kind of input and on the

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:12.700
output. Then there are the methodological challenges.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:18.168
And the biggest one we faced was the fact if the judge releases someone, we can see what happened.

00:13:18.168 --> 00:13:20.934
If the judge detains someone for trial, we don't know what would have happened.

00:13:20.934 --> 00:13:23.005
 You have to make it a counterfactual argument.

00:13:23.005 --> 00:13:26.689
 Exactly, counterfactual argument. The more we thought about the more we realized,

00:13:26.689 --> 00:13:33.124
this is a feature of many domains. Like in the sort of decision-making, the medical domain,

00:13:33.124 --> 00:13:38.871
which you're very familiar with. If the doctor applies a treatment or doesn't apply a treatment,

00:13:38.871 --> 00:13:43.076
we're now in two different worlds and we only get certain labels about things. If I choose,

00:13:43.076 --> 00:13:46.703
if I have -- if I choose to hire someone, I see how they

00:13:46.703 --> 00:13:49.247
moved on at my company. If I choose not to hire them,

00:13:49.247 --> 00:13:53.376
they go somewhere else, I'm not going to know how they would have done in the environment of my company.

00:13:53.376 --> 00:13:56.271
Often we get this one-sided label. It's not just randomly one-sided,

00:13:56.271 --> 00:13:59.304
it's based on the decision the decision-maker themselves made.

00:13:59.304 --> 00:14:04.243
The question is how do we demonstrate some kind of a gain or not through

00:14:04.243 --> 00:14:08.164
algorithms, if we only have labels in that kind of situation.

00:14:08.164 --> 00:14:15.837
And so at the core of what we were doing was to exploit some natural variation where we could actually --

00:14:15.837 --> 00:14:21.718
and so the kind of -- the sort of few-sentence version of the trick that's at the

00:14:21.718 --> 00:14:26.672
heart of the paper is that in many things you have defendants sort as if randomly,

00:14:26.672 --> 00:14:31.412
quasi randomly assigned to multiple judges who differ in their rates of

00:14:31.412 --> 00:14:34.894
leniency. So I have a judge who releases 80 out of every 100 people,

00:14:34.894 --> 00:14:37.809
or I have a judge who releases 90 out of every 100 people.

00:14:37.809 --> 00:14:40.401
Now I have a new way of releasing 80 out of 100 people,

00:14:40.401 --> 00:14:42.407
which is I can send them to the judge who releases 90 out of

00:14:42.407 --> 00:14:45.889
100, sort them all by risk as computed by the algorithm,

00:14:45.889 --> 00:14:52.214
use the algorithm to detain the ten people it finds the riskiest in that set of 90. The judge releases 90.

00:14:52.214 --> 00:14:57.492
We contract it back to 80. Now we have a set of 80 defendants who we detained. Now we have two

00:14:57.492 --> 00:15:02.291
ways to detain 80 defendants: We send them to the judge who is 80 percent lenient or we do this

00:15:02.291 --> 00:15:06.576
hybrid human algorithmic rule where the human overshoots and the algorithm scales it back.

00:15:06.576 --> 00:15:11.451
Now it's now an apples-to-apples comparison in some sense. And there we could

00:15:11.451 --> 00:15:15.831
demonstrate actually there's a lower crime rate on the hybrid human algorithmic rule,

00:15:15.831 --> 00:15:18.754
that somehow the worst decisions the judge is making are

00:15:18.754 --> 00:15:21.079
pretty simply bad and if you could simply fix those,

00:15:21.079 --> 00:15:24.714
you could leave most of the judge's decisions completely intact and basically improve at the margins.

00:15:24.714 --> 00:15:29.140
It's sort of intriguing to us because it was an interesting alternative

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:32.273
to the dichotomy between should we have humans do the work;

00:15:32.273 --> 00:15:36.294
should we fully automate? That's not really a binary decision. It's really that

00:15:36.294 --> 00:15:39.497
there are cases where the algorithm can be improving things at the margin.

00:15:39.497 --> 00:15:42.909
You can say I'm going to come in and fix the worst decisions. It's

00:15:42.909 --> 00:15:47.728
almost somewhere in one of our papers we make this analogy to it's --

00:15:47.728 --> 00:15:51.619
in the realm of cars we think about human drivers or self-driving

00:15:51.619 --> 00:15:54.293
cars. But of course long before we ever got near self-driving cars,

00:15:54.293 --> 00:15:57.406
we had all sorts of algorithmic assistance computer assistance in the

00:15:57.406 --> 00:16:02.104
vehicle that sort of is preventing you from making the worst mistakes.

00:16:02.104 --> 00:16:04.225
 Emergency braking situations.

00:16:04.225 --> 00:16:07.760
 Exactly. Emergency braking. Parking assist.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.895
 Yesterday, I'm not sure when this video will be watched, but just yesterday

00:16:10.895 --> 00:16:18.817
we had a report of an Uber self-driving car with human oversight killing a pedestrian,

00:16:18.817 --> 00:16:23.577
unfortunately, and it's kind of a landmark in this kind of thing. It's the kind of

00:16:23.577 --> 00:16:29.054
situation we've been worried about with automation being applied in the world.

00:16:29.054 --> 00:16:34.232
And in some discussions people said, oh, the cars will

00:16:34.232 --> 00:16:39.191
likely save many lives, I think over 36,000 last year,

00:16:39.191 --> 00:16:44.768
deaths and over a million injuries in the United States alone. And over a million worldwide,

00:16:44.768 --> 00:16:47.911
we hear deaths from automobile accidents, and certainly these cars

00:16:47.911 --> 00:16:53.349
will, even if they have these rough edges will be saving lives,

00:16:53.349 --> 00:16:56.472
but there's the sense when it's an automated system, there's a high

00:16:56.472 --> 00:17:00.243
standard we have for how it behaves and the values it represents.

00:17:00.243 --> 00:17:09.066
Thoughts on where we're going with these kinds of automation and any safety clerical areas.

00:17:09.066 --> 00:17:14.790
 This is a fascinating debate. Should we hold algorithms to a higher

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:19.869
standard. And on the one hand something appealing about saying that,

00:17:19.869 --> 00:17:22.124
we should hold algorithms to a higher standard. On the

00:17:22.124 --> 00:17:24.587
other hand, you're giving up something really tangible.

00:17:24.587 --> 00:17:27.671
There are a lot of lives that eventually will be saved if you

00:17:27.671 --> 00:17:30.117
decided not to hold algorithms to quite as a a high standard.

00:17:30.117 --> 00:17:36.880
You're paying for this decision at a high cost. And I think that's what makes this so tricky.

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:44.423
I do think it's interesting -- I mean I've been sort of trying to understand some about the issue of why --

00:17:44.423 --> 00:17:53.609
I think I do, and everyone I talk to feels this particularly strong reaction to the algorithm,

00:17:53.609 --> 00:18:03.295
the algorithm caused harm. The algorithm did something. And I think if we take

00:18:03.295 --> 00:18:06.882
some of the sort of pedagogical examples where we see algorithms causing harm,

00:18:06.882 --> 00:18:12.449
where the algorithm is in a no win situation, swerve left or right, it's going to kill or hurt someone.

00:18:12.449 --> 00:18:15.831
In those kind of situations, which are awful to contemplate,

00:18:15.831 --> 00:18:18.380
humans in those situations, where it's an awful situation,

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:23.853
it's very hard to say why they did what they did. Something happened very fast, tried their best,

00:18:23.853 --> 00:18:28.054
made essentially a random decision, something terrible happened; it was an accident, as we call it,

00:18:28.054 --> 00:18:32.543
right? One problem with the algorithms of course, we can go in

00:18:32.543 --> 00:18:36.105
and we can find millisecond by millisecond what it was doing,

00:18:36.105 --> 00:18:40.226
why it decided to turn left and not right, what was it actually thinking about. And

00:18:40.226 --> 00:18:44.397
I feel like if we step back since we always tend to anthropomorphize these things,

00:18:44.397 --> 00:18:49.256
we attach more moral opprobrium to things that are premeditated,

00:18:49.256 --> 00:18:52.049
things you thought it through and did the bad thing anyway.

00:18:52.049 --> 00:18:57.876
And the problem is everything that the algorithm does, most of these algorithms does are premeditated,

00:18:57.876 --> 00:19:01.647
because we can see what it was thinking; whereas, with the human at some level

00:19:01.647 --> 00:19:03.826
we'll never know what they were thinking they were trying their best --

00:19:03.826 --> 00:19:07.135
 Under bounded resources, all in real time.

00:19:07.135 --> 00:19:07.704
 Exactly.

00:19:07.704 --> 00:19:09.414
 Using best intuitions.

00:19:09.414 --> 00:19:14.478
 Exactly. And some of the algorithm was somehow engaging in the calculation

00:19:14.478 --> 00:19:17.531
about things where we think it's morally questionable to engage a calculation.

00:19:17.531 --> 00:19:21.173
That I think is part of the reason why we ascribe --

00:19:21.173 --> 00:19:27.668
 Makes it also certainly, brings it into salience the fact that at the moments

00:19:27.668 --> 00:19:33.335
of decision-making you see uncertainty represented potentially and an action that was taken and

00:19:33.335 --> 00:19:37.469
an objective function that's clear and you can state it now in a sensitive machine somewhere.

00:19:37.469 --> 00:19:41.597
Someone said here's the trade you make in this situation; here's the cost and benefits.

00:19:41.597 --> 00:19:42.135
 Yeah.

00:19:42.135 --> 00:19:47.164
 So that explicitness I think also brings up questions where the

00:19:47.164 --> 00:19:49.984
question should have been to begin with even with implicit decision-making.

00:19:49.984 --> 00:19:56.906
 That's the question. And of course you've thought a lot about these issues. But I do feel like a

00:19:56.906 --> 00:20:02.090
lot of people genuinely feel like even having thought about it that there's some advantage to living in a

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:11.239
world of strategic ambiguity where we never actually calculate the choice between two terrible alternatives.

00:20:11.239 --> 00:20:19.318
 So you've done many pieces of work, a lot of them, as you said, at these interesting intersections,

00:20:19.318 --> 00:20:23.122
looking at communities, how communities form on the Web and their nature and

00:20:23.122 --> 00:20:28.270
structure, how links get formed and the power of linked prediction and so on.

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:33.618
And we see daily now in our various services like Facebook and LinkedIn,

00:20:33.618 --> 00:20:37.869
some comments on directions there and why that's so exciting.

00:20:37.869 --> 00:20:43.626
 I mean, if I sort of look over the sort of longer history of this,

00:20:43.626 --> 00:20:49.113
I feel like if I back up to where we were talking about Web search,

00:20:49.113 --> 00:20:55.769
circa '96, '97, I think at the time the conceptualization I had was

00:20:55.769 --> 00:20:58.911
the Web is this vast universal library of all human knowledge.

00:20:58.911 --> 00:21:03.924
You go and you dip in the library and you pull out knowledge from it. I feel like there was

00:21:03.924 --> 00:21:09.610
a period 2004-2006 was a sort of really important period in the development of the Web.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:15.433
It was really a moment when somehow the level of participation, the technology, the bandwidth had

00:21:15.433 --> 00:21:19.285
reached where people could actually step out from behind their Web pages and interact more directly.

00:21:19.285 --> 00:21:23.876
It was no longer I interact with you because my Web page linked to your Web page. We're directly commenting

00:21:23.876 --> 00:21:28.134
on each other's content and tagging each other's content and collaboratively authoring something.

00:21:28.134 --> 00:21:33.344
 It's kind of interactive phase transition or experiential phase transition.

00:21:33.344 --> 00:21:38.391
 Exactly. A lot of the things we now take as sort

00:21:38.391 --> 00:21:41.363
of pillars of the current Web all came about right there.

00:21:41.363 --> 00:21:49.835
In that two to three-year period you see the creation of Facebook, YouTube, Twitter. All of them sort of --

00:21:49.835 --> 00:21:55.808
all of this social media sort of kind of speciating sort of really quickly in that period.

00:21:55.808 --> 00:22:00.900
 I want to mention I remember a time being interviewed by somebody on the work we did,

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:09.171
which is of your interest as well, with Jure Leskovec on theory separation and it created a flurry.

00:22:09.171 --> 00:22:22.242
I remember trying to explain to a well-known moderator on I think it was on CNBC what Facebook was,

00:22:22.242 --> 00:22:25.835
what social media was. And I remember having to come up with like:

00:22:25.835 --> 00:22:29.495
Let me explain this in 20 words or less but it had to be explained.

00:22:29.495 --> 00:22:34.583
 Yeah, it's amazing, things move so fast that things that you could

00:22:34.583 --> 00:22:38.934
present as novelties three or four years later feel like common knowledge.

00:22:38.934 --> 00:22:48.383
And, yeah, I remember the first Google faculty summit they brought us all in

00:22:48.383 --> 00:22:51.231
there, we have this amazing thing called Gmail that offers a gigabyte of

00:22:51.231 --> 00:22:56.092
storage and on the horizon is an amazing new prototype called Google Maps.

00:22:56.092 --> 00:22:59.952
That was, what, only 12, 13 years ago.

00:22:59.952 --> 00:23:05.174
 Interesting how fast that has happened. But back to this idea of community and interaction.

00:23:05.174 --> 00:23:08.606
 Exactly. So I think that period suddenly people were interacting

00:23:08.606 --> 00:23:14.161
directly. And certainly that struck me as a very important moment.

00:23:14.161 --> 00:23:19.571
And even to the extent I've been thinking of the Web graph for sort of ten years up to that point,

00:23:19.571 --> 00:23:28.431
the idea of human social networks being reflected online I think it's been a very -- a very powerful thing.

00:23:28.431 --> 00:23:35.036
And it certainly brought about a new kind of information seeking behavior, a new way

00:23:35.036 --> 00:23:38.997
in which we engage with things where rather than just go out and look for things,

00:23:38.997 --> 00:23:45.552
it comes to you through your network of friends as it did in the offline world. And obviously it's sort of,

00:23:45.552 --> 00:23:49.133
anytime one talks about that now, one has to think about sort of the way it's evolved

00:23:49.133 --> 00:23:55.924
in the past few years where you now have this combination of your real friends from

00:23:55.924 --> 00:23:59.485
the offline world sharing information with you and commercial entities sharing

00:23:59.485 --> 00:24:03.063
information with you and all of that somehow happening in a big superposition,

00:24:03.063 --> 00:24:06.542
this is what you're being exposed to now online.

00:24:06.542 --> 00:24:12.384
 I want to come back to that topic in a second. But I want to route you through

00:24:12.384 --> 00:24:18.607
thinking about some of your contributions with your collaborators on incentives and Web activity.

00:24:18.607 --> 00:24:23.386
You've done some really fabulous work on badges, for example, and I'm trying

00:24:23.386 --> 00:24:29.442
to understand how different kinds of programs were reinforcing work,

00:24:29.442 --> 00:24:36.546
for example, say in a crowdsourcing system or system where people are helping each other, GitHub,

00:24:36.546 --> 00:24:45.855
for example. How you can actually shape what people do based on the design of the merit program.

00:24:45.855 --> 00:24:47.399
Can you say a little more about that?

00:24:47.399 --> 00:24:51.067
 Yes, so this was something that we became interested in because

00:24:51.067 --> 00:24:54.296
in the online world there became this proliferation of badges.

00:24:54.296 --> 00:24:57.259
If you do this much of activity you get a badge. If you do this

00:24:57.259 --> 00:25:00.293
much this frequently over some period of time you get a badge.

00:25:00.293 --> 00:25:03.714
These had incentivizing effects. People would set these as

00:25:03.714 --> 00:25:08.620
goals for themselves. And some work that Jure Leskovec,

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:10.539
Ashton Anderson and Dan Huttenlocher and I did, you

00:25:10.539 --> 00:25:13.432
actually see the effect, where people near the milestone,

00:25:13.432 --> 00:25:15.458
their activity increased. Then they hit the milestone and it

00:25:15.458 --> 00:25:19.139
dropped off. They're clearly aiming for that as the finish line.

00:25:19.139 --> 00:25:24.427
And you end up with this sort of interesting badge design problem or badge

00:25:24.427 --> 00:25:27.700
placement problem where it's almost as though you had people sort of steering

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:31.699
through the space of activities and in a way we actually first discovered it this,

00:25:31.699 --> 00:25:35.193
we were just looking at the activity as it were just organic activity and we saw

00:25:35.193 --> 00:25:39.411
everyone was kind of torquing in a strange direction in this part of the space.

00:25:39.411 --> 00:25:45.786
We all felt like astrophysicists saying there's clearly some gravitating body here that's bending the flow.

00:25:45.786 --> 00:25:48.378
We have to figure out what it is because it's invisible to us.

00:25:48.378 --> 00:25:49.368
 Dark matter.

00:25:49.368 --> 00:25:52.633
 And you go look there's a badge right there and

00:25:52.633 --> 00:25:55.455
everyone is bending around it as they try to get the badge.

00:25:55.455 --> 00:25:59.185
 You pick up your telescope, you see the badge coming into view with the scope.

00:25:59.185 --> 00:26:04.525
 Exactly. And you're actually -- like plot the flow lines and you would see this.

00:26:04.525 --> 00:26:05.503
So that becomes --

00:26:05.503 --> 00:26:10.392
 I always thought when I saw those figures in your paper, magnetic filings.

00:26:10.392 --> 00:26:17.984
 That's exactly right. And so what's nice is you can build some models in which it's actually,

00:26:17.984 --> 00:26:22.206
in retrospect, an intuitive model that if a badge is very far away, there's a good

00:26:22.206 --> 00:26:25.218
chance I'm going to leave the system before it ever becomes relevant to me.

00:26:25.218 --> 00:26:28.532
If I'm very close to a badge I'm probably going to make it. It actually

00:26:28.532 --> 00:26:32.402
acquires more value and actually has more drawing power the closer I get to it,

00:26:32.402 --> 00:26:39.014
almost like one of these physical objects. It becomes a design problem, where should I put the badges.

00:26:39.014 --> 00:26:44.773
So now it's up to me, I'm trying to sort of create some beautiful shape with the iron filings.

00:26:44.773 --> 00:26:49.403
I can put the magnets, bar magnets wherever I want. And it does

00:26:49.403 --> 00:26:52.626
have an effect. If I create an incentive that comes too soon,

00:26:52.626 --> 00:26:55.170
I get everyone to participate but pretty quickly they're

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:56.936
through the incentive and I've lost control of them.

00:26:56.936 --> 00:27:02.553
If I put it too far away, seems hopeless. There's some optimal distance at which to put the incentive.

00:27:02.553 --> 00:27:06.803
So it pulls people toward it in a way that's achievable. And so

00:27:06.803 --> 00:27:10.077
that's -- so I think there's a very interesting design space there.

00:27:10.077 --> 00:27:15.338
 Now I want to come back to an earlier topic. I told you I was

00:27:15.338 --> 00:27:21.077
going to go through incentives this idea of mixing ideas of incentives and design,

00:27:21.077 --> 00:27:31.377
oft we say well-intentioned designers who have users in mind or learnings or the community in mind when

00:27:31.377 --> 00:27:42.323
they do a design like this to other incentives and objectives to the point where we see the possibility

00:27:42.323 --> 00:27:49.162
in this new connected world with all sorts of entities including commercial entities to do large scale

00:27:49.162 --> 00:27:56.131
designs aimed at manipulation which might not necessarily be in line with the interests of the individuals.

00:27:56.131 --> 00:28:03.983
And the prospect, for example, and the possibility that some of the best machine learning people

00:28:03.983 --> 00:28:11.855
coming out of our departments and organizations now will go off and spend time with large scale

00:28:11.855 --> 00:28:18.381
datasets and machine learning algorithms to optimize time of engagement on a timeline,

00:28:18.381 --> 00:28:24.946
the duration, for example, or the other kinds of behaviors on the Web that might be aimed at commerce,

00:28:24.946 --> 00:28:33.077
at maximizing profits and therefore being sort of -- they're being great incentives by entoÿÿes

00:28:33.077 --> 00:28:39.148
to create algorithmic approaches to doing adversarial in some ways attacks on human attention.

00:28:39.148 --> 00:28:44.578
Where is this going and how might we address this potential problem?

00:28:44.578 --> 00:28:48.362
 So that's obviously an interesting question. And here I think the challenge

00:28:48.362 --> 00:28:54.090
is this problem is so hard that I sort of hesitate to say anything that sounds

00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:58.669
prescriptive because I really don't think I've thought hard about it enough to really --

00:28:58.669 --> 00:29:02.787
and I've thought pretty hard about it. But I just feel like it's an incredibly hard thing.

00:29:02.787 --> 00:29:10.233
I should say that it is not a completely new issue. So as a child, I watched

00:29:10.233 --> 00:29:13.825
a lot of beer commercials because I liked watching football and baseball,

00:29:13.825 --> 00:29:17.237
and because I liked football and baseball and I was age eight, I also saw lots of beer commercials.

00:29:17.237 --> 00:29:22.585
You could say was that the socially optimal allocation of people's

00:29:22.585 --> 00:29:26.002
attention. But it was very much the state of the world,

00:29:26.002 --> 00:29:32.580
adversarial attack. Most of the professional sports was underwritten ultimately by advertising.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:37.996
If you liked professional sports or any kind of entertainment, you would see lots of advertising.

00:29:37.996 --> 00:29:42.150
And often advertising not particularly well targeted to your particular interest.

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:49.364
And I think that was some of the optimism around online media that the things

00:29:49.364 --> 00:29:52.247
that are going to be generating revenue ought to at least be tailored to things

00:29:52.247 --> 00:29:55.975
appropriate for you and in the end isn't that actually a better kind of advertising.

00:29:55.975 --> 00:30:00.977
So I think we've always created media that tries to

00:30:00.977 --> 00:30:03.144
optimize for engagement, tries to optimize for attention.

00:30:03.144 --> 00:30:05.991
We've tended to do it with more qualitative tools.

00:30:05.991 --> 00:30:09.963
 Like, for example, when we were growing up even now, an episode

00:30:09.963 --> 00:30:15.584
of Twilight Zone would hit that moment and someone would say we'll be right back,

00:30:15.584 --> 00:30:16.512
don't go away.

00:30:16.512 --> 00:30:26.013
 Exactly, yeah. And all of these were sort of lower data attempts to get -- so I

00:30:26.013 --> 00:30:30.500
don't think in terms of the intent something new is happening that wasn't happening then.

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:38.412
I think as you're saying what has changed is that there's much more data and therefore much more ability

00:30:38.412 --> 00:30:49.298
to do really sort of surgically precise interventions but I think it's an activity that has sort of been

00:30:49.298 --> 00:30:55.203
true for as long as sort of humans have created media to attract the attentions of other human beings.

00:30:55.203 --> 00:31:04.792
But at some point quantity becomes quality. And so I think it does bring definitely new issues into play.

00:31:04.792 --> 00:31:12.335
And the extent to which we should ask to what extent is people's immediate

00:31:12.335 --> 00:31:15.375
interest in seeking out what kind of activates or energizes them,

00:31:15.375 --> 00:31:22.253
to what extent should we trade that off with long-term gain. And that's not just a societal interest.

00:31:22.253 --> 00:31:27.930
Even for the platforms themselves. In the end platforms are perhaps

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:31.234
less worried about whether you spend the next one hour on the platform,

00:31:31.234 --> 00:31:35.227
whether you remain an engaged participant over the long term. If you're doing something

00:31:35.227 --> 00:31:39.374
in the short term that keeps people emotionally aroused and on the platform,

00:31:39.374 --> 00:31:45.071
but in the long term they feel bad about the experience, that's neither in society's interest,

00:31:45.071 --> 00:31:46.049
nor is it in the platform's interest.

00:31:46.049 --> 00:31:51.197
 I guess you can imagine a concern being you do both, you arouse them and get their interest long term.

00:31:51.197 --> 00:31:57.912
And five years from now someone looks back and says, boy, I wish I spent my time in a better way.

00:31:57.912 --> 00:32:03.559
And so there is this idea of some movement or some

00:32:03.559 --> 00:32:07.560
reflection at level of industry or society more generally,

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:12.289
civil liberties organizations, a time well spent and what does that mean and when does

00:32:12.289 --> 00:32:17.477
that run counter to commercial goals with their precision manipulation slash attack

00:32:17.477 --> 00:32:23.454
slash optimization of how they would like to have people spend their attention and time.

00:32:23.454 --> 00:32:27.054
 No, I think it's -- yeah, this is a very important issue which I've -- I've

00:32:27.054 --> 00:32:33.547
been thinking about it more and more because it's clearly on everyone's minds.

00:32:33.547 --> 00:32:37.901
But it is useful to think back to everything that was said about TV. And

00:32:37.901 --> 00:32:41.760
like my generation was supposedly ruined by all the TV that we watched,

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:48.046
all the kind of mindless entertainment, the violent entertainment, all the commercials we were exposed to.

00:32:48.046 --> 00:32:54.752
This was why our country was falling behind all the other countries. I got an enormous diet of this kind of

00:32:54.752 --> 00:33:01.547
worry and anxiety in the 1980s and it was because there was a media interest motivated by similar things;

00:33:01.547 --> 00:33:06.127
they needed to keep an audience that was engaged. I think as we think about

00:33:06.127 --> 00:33:08.731
this, I think it's useful to separate it into the parts that are new,

00:33:08.731 --> 00:33:14.512
which is how data assists and computation assisted it, is from the parts that old, which is the kind of urge

00:33:14.512 --> 00:33:22.729
people providing media and providing content to keep their audiences engaged in ways that worry some of us.

00:33:22.729 --> 00:33:29.012
 Changing topics to maybe friendlier ones, something

00:33:29.012 --> 00:33:33.904
less serious, but still important in terms of time well spent.

00:33:33.904 --> 00:33:39.042
When I visited you, gave a talk in 1999, Cornell, gave a keynote and you

00:33:39.042 --> 00:33:45.527
hosted me. And I remember when you were dropping me at the airport,

00:33:45.527 --> 00:33:50.326
we're back at the hotel, wherever I was going. You had your big bags full of hockey equipment,

00:33:50.326 --> 00:33:53.958
you and your wife. And you were going to go out to play ice hockey. Do you still play ice hockey?

00:33:53.958 --> 00:33:59.116
 I play it less and less. At the time we played

00:33:59.116 --> 00:34:01.311
twice a week. And I remember talking about it with you.

00:34:01.311 --> 00:34:06.774
And we actually should have had you play when you were there. I think we learned

00:34:06.774 --> 00:34:10.061
from our mistake because a couple years later we invited John Lafferty.

00:34:10.061 --> 00:34:11.548
 I hear he's a good player.

00:34:11.548 --> 00:34:15.469
 Incredibly good player. And we didn't know that at the time but he says he plays hockey.

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:19.081
We said perfect. He gave the colloquium, went to a quick

00:34:19.081 --> 00:34:22.074
dinner, didn't eat too much, brought him to the hockey.

00:34:22.074 --> 00:34:25.634
He had to borrow skates that didn't quite fit and a helmet.

00:34:25.634 --> 00:34:26.513
 Skates.

00:34:26.513 --> 00:34:32.510
 Exactly. Despite that, the instant he stepped on the ice, oh wait, he's really good.

00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:35.994
Because you can tell in a few seconds if someone is really good at this.

00:34:35.994 --> 00:34:40.915
Definitely. That was a phase we were both playing twice a week.

00:34:40.915 --> 00:34:46.399
 I'm about to give a talk soon at Cornell. I'll take you up on this potentially.

00:34:46.399 --> 00:34:51.139
 But I should add the sad part to this is that over

00:34:51.139 --> 00:34:55.937
time we've definitely been -- and obviously you sort of,

00:34:55.937 --> 00:34:59.519
becoming parents adds new time constraints and getting busier.

00:34:59.519 --> 00:35:02.249
And then I reached a point where I also fear injury.

00:35:02.249 --> 00:35:08.568
I mean I would say in our annual intramural league somebody would get nontrivially injured at

00:35:08.568 --> 00:35:14.134
least once and I'm definitely keenly aware I don't recover from things as fast these days.

00:35:14.134 --> 00:35:22.859
 I play ice hockey also. Typically it's on a once-a-week team and I have to say you watch popcorn,

00:35:22.859 --> 00:35:25.440
like a Poisson process, injuries will occur. And once in a while

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:29.491
you walk out with an injury and takes a while to recover.

00:35:29.491 --> 00:35:34.692
And as you say as you get older, things take a little bit longer to reset.

00:35:34.692 --> 00:35:34.813
 Yeah.

00:35:34.813 --> 00:35:44.076
 A few comments about, you talked a little bit about your background, having fun with the

00:35:44.076 --> 00:35:51.860
Apple 2 and finding out this is actually a field and having a place to leverage your math and your inclination

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:57.747
to see math actually execute in the real world and have influence and help to interpret the real world.

00:35:57.747 --> 00:36:03.643
What kind of advice would you give to, let's say, undergraduates now maybe

00:36:03.643 --> 00:36:08.532
finishing up their undergrad careers and how to think about next steps?

00:36:08.532 --> 00:36:11.883
 That's a great question. And I mean certainly something all of

00:36:11.883 --> 00:36:15.337
us have been thrilled at the level of interest in computer science.

00:36:15.337 --> 00:36:21.662
It's just been growing rapidly and it's enormous over the past few years. I think one

00:36:21.662 --> 00:36:28.347
is to really just think about how broadly the ideas of computing can be applied.

00:36:28.347 --> 00:36:35.142
I think almost every area either is being influenced by the ideas of computation or could be.

00:36:35.142 --> 00:36:40.849
And so I think it's a great opportunity to really take this as a fundamental body of

00:36:40.849 --> 00:36:44.106
knowledge that you can combine with almost anything else that you find interesting.

00:36:44.106 --> 00:36:48.860
As I said, in eighth grade I wanted to be a lawyer when I grew up. And it took me a very

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:52.783
long time to finally come back and sort of combine the computation with that interest,

00:36:52.783 --> 00:37:02.450
and I was sort of late to the party; people were doing it all along. But I think if you have an interest,

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:06.081
it's almost sure to be compatible with ideas from computing

00:37:06.081 --> 00:37:08.725
in some way and that's just how broad the field has become.

00:37:08.725 --> 00:37:15.061
And then I think the other thing that I try to advise students, it's easy to see in the

00:37:15.061 --> 00:37:20.289
media how we live in an era of big science and all science can be done by giant teams and

00:37:20.289 --> 00:37:24.829
your future is to be the person who contributes one tiny data point on some giant plot.

00:37:24.829 --> 00:37:28.002
And I think I can't speak for the other sciences and what that

00:37:28.002 --> 00:37:30.701
looks like, but in computer science what I think is so exciting,

00:37:30.701 --> 00:37:34.014
there's still exciting discoveries to be made. You and the

00:37:34.014 --> 00:37:37.809
manageable dataset and your laptop and some new conceptual idea.

00:37:37.809 --> 00:37:45.671
And a lot of stuff happens still in groups of two or three or one, with very modest resources.

00:37:45.671 --> 00:37:48.800
And there's still amazing things to be discovered there. Obviously there's

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:53.683
also the stuff that happens on big datacenters and some of it needs that.

00:37:53.683 --> 00:37:57.135
And if that's what you're interested in, there's room to work in that style.

00:37:57.135 --> 00:38:01.256
But it's sort of nice to know that someone working just with a friend of

00:38:01.256 --> 00:38:05.726
theirs on a laptop can still do things that attract everyone's attention.

00:38:05.726 --> 00:38:11.752
And that's also a very appealing thing about the field; that the important problems are still accessible

00:38:11.752 --> 00:38:16.826
and some of them are quite manageable for people who are undergrads or just going to college.

00:38:16.826 --> 00:38:20.635
 That's a great place to stop. Well, thanks very much. Enjoyed talking to you.

00:38:20.635 --> 00:38:21.679
 Thanks so much. Good talking to you.

00:38:21.679 --> 00:38:26.115
 It's a wrap. How was that?

00:38:26.115 --> 00:38:28.410
 It was fantastic.

